/-!
# Generated mixed-plane paths and strict whole-radius growth

The first edge of a small critical path has the critical transposition in
its mixed-plane image. In the six-letter quotient, an explicit crossing
conjugate of that plane satisfies two simultaneous requirements: the two
planes generate S₆, and the normalizer of the new plane generates S₆ with
the critical transposition. Short permutation words certify this choice.
Lifting through the literal projection and free-amalgam embedding selects
one actual edge satisfying both requirements. Relative-normalizer escape
then gives a strict backward critical path whose first interior stabilizer
is generated by its two incident full edge kernels. Both transporters and
all shared vertices of the original walk are retained.

For such a selected path, the two incident kernels intersect in the actual
component two-core. Thus the full opposite radius kernel in the starting
core lies in the first interior core. If the opposite radius has index
four, its kernel tower has two steps of index two, and its intermediate
kernel is precisely the endpoint seed joined with the original radius
kernel. Its commutator with the starting seed is consequently the full
critical commutator. The existing endpoint-escape theorem on the reversed
walk contradicts this equality. The small-radius lower bound upgrades
this to strict index greater than four.

The actual backward-path consumer discharges the interior generation
premise, and transport supplies the same selected large-radius path at
every original component vertex. This is the small whole-radius exclusion
in the mixed-plane part of MS93 (5.11)(1), not the remaining large-radius
terminal contradiction. Relative-normalizer generation alone does not
supply generation by two incident edge kernels; the simultaneous choice
above is essential. No full-core estimate is inferred from a selected
span estimate. Both moved covers, all central attachments, the original
radical versus replacement distinction, and the literal natural graph and
quotient maps are preserved. Larger original-Sylow images, distance four,
LargeDistanceInput and unconditional replacement distance two remain open.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher, Pushing up weak BN-pairs of rank two
(1993), the simultaneous selections in (5.5)--(5.7), pp.915--919, and the
kernel tower and small-radius exclusion of (5.11)(1), pp.923--924. Private
imports reuse the checked local selection and kernel-tower lemmas without
changing their earlier public boundaries.
-/

